From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Norman Herms <dockseed@proton.me>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 18:00:21 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] drm/sun4i: Add the A733 DisplayPort transmitter

The A733 has a DisplayPort 1.4 / eDP 1.4 transmitter with one, two or
four lanes at up to 5.4 Gb/s per lane, fed by TCON TV1 (user manual
section 15.4). On the Cubie A7S it reaches the USB Type-C connector in
alternate mode, and the hot plug state comes from the port controller.

Add a driver in the form of the sun4i HDMI encoder: a component that
creates the encoder of its TCON channel, a bridge, and the connector of
its bridge chain. Its link and AUX registers are those of the Xilinx
ZynqMP DisplayPort controller, but stream, reset and PHY control differ
and that driver is bound to its display subsystem. This patch has the
AUX channel, detection, EDID and mode validation; the link follows.

AUX requests follow section 15.4.7; the end of a request is taken from
the latched reply bits, cleared before each request. The AUX divider is
the bus clock in MHz, and the AUX PHY is powered while a sink is
reported, or for the one request that looks for it. Modes must fit the
link at 8 bits per component. Pixel clocks above 154 MHz, the highest
this pipe has carried, are refused; that is a tested value, not a limit
of the hardware. Whether the TCON can make the pixel clock of a mode is
checked by its CRTC.

Seen on the Radxa Cubie A7S: with the monitor already plugged in when the
driver binds, the connector stayed disconnected and no mode was found.
Without a probe in detect, the transmitter knows the hot plug state only
from the port controller, which reports it on a change, and the DRM core
drops a report for which no connector is registered yet. Nothing sends it
again, so the state stays wrong while the sink is there. When no hot plug
is reported, detect therefore powers the AUX PHY and makes one DPCD
read of the revision; an answer means a sink, and the next report of the
port controller replaces that state. On a port without a sink the DRM
helper retries that read until it gives up.

The sink capabilities are copied under the driver lock, which the AUX
transfer takes itself, so the read is made outside it. The option
depends on PM: the bus clock and the reset come with runtime PM.
For system sleep the runtime state is forced in the late and early
phases, so the DRM core disables the bridge in the normal phase before
the block is gated and enables it again after it is ungated, whatever
the order of the display engine and the transmitter on the PM list.

Differences from the vendor code: the AUX divider follows the bus clock,
an AUX request is sent only to a reported sink or to look for one, and
the channel is a DRM AUX device.

Tested on Radxa Cubie A7S: booted on one board; the sink's capabilities
and EDID were read, the mode list stayed the same over repeated forced
detects running alongside the picture, and the link came up with
enhanced framing; after an s2idle cycle the link came back and the sink
saw the same picture as before.

Assisted-by: LLM
